From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] PCI: let the gaokun3 NVMe link go down in system suspend

On these Qualcomm hosts the nvme driver already shuts the disk down on
every system suspend (broken_l1ss_resume), but the link stays up:
dw_pcie_suspend_noirq() only sends PME_Turn_Off when
pci_host_common_d3cold_possible() agrees, and that wants every device
under the host bridge in D3hot. On gaokun3 the Root Ports are still in
D0 when the controller suspends, so the check never passes, and the
controller, its PHY and clocks and the disk's 3.3 V supply stay on
through every sleep.

A Root Port is part of the controller and goes down with it. Ignore it
when only NVMe controllers sit below it, and let the endpoints decide
as before. Other links are left alone; the Wi-Fi one has to stay up for
WoWLAN.

With the link down, qcom_pcie_suspend_noirq() drops the controller's
vote for DDR bandwidth. Once no master votes for DDR during s2idle this
tablet hangs until the watchdog resets it, also when both links are
taken down by other means, while a vote of 1 kB/s avoids that. Keep
that token vote instead of disabling the path.

This is a workaround for gaokun3: why the Root Ports stay in D0, and
why dropping the last DDR vote hangs the SoC, are both still open.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c   |  7 +++++-
 drivers/pci/controller/pci-host-common.c | 27 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 33 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
index d62cf302d..a213d3f07 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/dwc/pcie-qcom.c
@@ -2207,7 +2207,12 @@ static int qcom_pcie_suspend_noirq(struct device *dev)
 		return ret;
 
 	if (pcie->pci->suspended) {
-		ret = icc_disable(pcie->icc_mem);
+		/*
+		 * gaokun3: keep a token vote for DDR bandwidth with the link
+		 * down. Once no master votes for DDR during s2idle, the tablet
+		 * hangs until the watchdog resets it.
+		 */
+		ret = icc_set_bw(pcie->icc_mem, 0, kBps_to_icc(1));
 		if (ret)
 			dev_err(dev, "Failed to disable PCIe-MEM interconnect path: %d\n", ret);
 
diff --git a/drivers/pci/controller/pci-host-common.c b/drivers/pci/controller/pci-host-common.c
index 2ce6f4b66..5cac27633 100644
--- a/drivers/pci/controller/pci-host-common.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/pci-host-common.c
@@ -283,11 +283,38 @@ void pci_host_common_remove(struct platform_device *pdev)
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(pci_host_common_remove);
 
+/*
+ * gaokun3: the Root Ports are still in D0 when the controller suspends, so
+ * the check below never lets a link go down. A Root Port is part of the
+ * controller and goes down with it, so let the endpoints decide where they
+ * are all NVMe controllers: on these hosts the nvme driver shuts the disk
+ * down on every suspend anyway (broken_l1ss_resume). Leave other links
+ * alone, the Wi-Fi one has to stay up for WoWLAN.
+ */
+static bool pci_host_common_nvme_only(struct pci_dev *port)
+{
+	struct pci_dev *dev;
+
+	if (!port->subordinate || list_empty(&port->subordinate->devices))
+		return false;
+
+	/* pci_walk_bus() holds pci_bus_sem */
+	list_for_each_entry(dev, &port->subordinate->devices, bus_list)
+		if (dev->class != PCI_CLASS_STORAGE_EXPRESS)
+			return false;
+
+	return true;
+}
+
 static int __pci_host_common_d3cold_possible(struct pci_dev *pdev,
 					     void *userdata)
 {
 	u32 *flags = userdata;
 
+	if (pci_pcie_type(pdev) == PCI_EXP_TYPE_ROOT_PORT &&
+	    pci_host_common_nvme_only(pdev))
+		return 0;
+
 	if (!pdev->dev.driver && !pci_is_enabled(pdev))
 		return 0;
 
